{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And He will see the blood on the lintel and on the two doorposts.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): since Israel was the cause for publicizing the divinity in the world through this redemption from Egypt, therefore they were commanded in the matter of the paschal offering to perform acts of publicity against the wicked king under whose authority they were — to disgrace the faith of the Egyptians before their very eyes, and to establish in the heart faith in the Holy One, blessed be He, and fear of Him. From this same principle they were commanded to slaughter the Egyptians' deities and to eat it roasted, not boiled in a pot, but roast fire — so that the matter would be revealed and publicized to all who entered the houses, and even to passersby its fragrance would be publicized. And in order to publicize the matter further, they were commanded to place of the blood on the lintel and on the doorposts, which are exposed and publicized places, to indicate that the attribute of judgment was stretched out against the Egyptians, in the manner of what is written (Amos 9:1): ״הך הכפתור וירעשו הספים״ and so forth." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah you need to know that this verse alludes to the letters of the special Name, and it mentioned this above by way of building construction — that is, it said ״upon the two doorposts and upon the lintel,״ for thus is the way of one who sets up a building, to set first the doorposts and afterward to place the lintel upon them; but now it preceded the lintel and afterward the two doorposts. And from here you will understand the secret of the verse (Proverbs 8:34): ״אשרי אדם שומע לי לשקוד על דלתותי יום יום לשמור מזוזות פתחי״, and also the statement of Rav Ḥisda: a person should always pass through two doorways and afterward pray — two doorways? Can you imagine? Rather say: the measure of two doorways. And I shall add an explanation in this verse: ״ועבר ה׳ לנגוף את מצרים וראה את הדם״ — the Name in its fullness, and this is ״והיה הדם לכם לאות״, from the language of letters: upon the lintel the ו״ה, and upon the two doorposts the two הי״ן. This is <b>what it says: ופסח ה׳ על הפתח,</b> in the language of striding, that is, He will skip from there; <b>ולא יתן המשחית לבא,</b> into it, because it is sealed with His seal. And understand this. And if so, there were four altars corresponding to the four letters of the Name. And so they said in the Midrash: four altars did our ancestors have in Egypt, and they are the lintel and the two doorposts and the threshold, and the threshold was a small round vessel. And likewise the four cups of Passover correspond to them: the first cup, of Kiddush, corresponds to the letter יו״ד, which is קדש (sanctity), and thus it says (Psalms 134:2): ״שאו ידיכם קדש״; the second cup, over which one recites the Haggadah, which is the telling of the miracles, corresponds to the letter ה״א, from which the miracles and the plagues are drawn; the third cup, of grace after meals, corresponds to the letter וי״ו, which is called שמים (heaven) from what is written (I Kings 8:32): ״ואתה תשמע השמים״, and from there comes sustenance, from what is written (Exodus 16:4): ״הנני ממטיר לכם לחם מן השמים״; the fourth cup, over which one says ״שפוך חמתך״, corresponds to the final ה״א, which is the attribute of judgment. And this is clear." "<b>And it is possible further</b> according to the plain sense (peshat) that the sign of the blood alluded to life and death: death to their enemies, namely the plague of the firstborn, and life to Israel, for the blood is the soul; and once this remembrance was performed upon them, ״ולא יתן המשחית״. And the meaning of this Destroyer is the angel of destruction; it should not be understood as referring to the blow struck in Egypt, for the Holy One, blessed be He, in His own Person and glory, is the One who strikes. And the verse explained ״ופסח ה׳״ — that He Himself is the One striking in Egypt — ״על הפתח ולא יתן״ permission to the angel of destruction in the world at the time of the plague to enter their houses, in the manner of what He said concerning David (II Samuel 24:16): ״ויאמר למלאך המשחית בעם רב עתה הרף ידך״. And this verse teaches us that even though the Holy One, blessed be He, is the One who strikes, the striking angel was there — he and his many forces, all of them forces of the attribute of judgment appointed over judgment — like a king who passes from place to place to take vengeance on his enemies, and there come with him speculators, but those speculators were not permitted to strike at all, nor did they find their hands free to act against the designated ones on that night in Egypt, for this restraint came to them from the Holy One, blessed be He, who did not wish to exact vengeance except through Himself; and therefore they were not permitted to destroy in Egypt, for He, blessed be He, restrained them from this, in the manner of what they expounded: ״אני ולא מלאך, אני ולא שרף, אני ולא השליח.״ But over Israel there is no such restraint, and it was possible that one of the destructive forces might prevail over them, and therefore they needed the sign of the blood — for without the sign they would have destroyed; and this is what it says: ״ולא יתן המשחית,״ that is, and He will not give permission to the angel of destruction to enter your houses. And what is understood from this is that without the sign he would have come, since it was a matter of possibility it was not prevented, as over Egypt; but from now on it would be prevented by virtue of the sign."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The comment stating ו״ה upon the lintel and הי״ן upon the doorposts is translated exactly as written; the letter assignments are left as R. Bahya gives them without verification."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:30.439350853Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}